nt cio, which is here.  *answer        reply to messages in a folder in turn  *arepdaemon    the daemon autoreply relies on  *autoreply     send an automatic reply while you are away  *checkalias    check an alias resolves before you rely on it  *elm           the Elm mail reader itself -- full-screen, menu-driven  *fastmail      send a file as mail without opening the reader  *filter        sort incoming mail into folders by rule  *frm           list who your mail is from, one line each  *listalias     list the aliases you have  *messages      count and list what is in a folder  *newalias      rebuild the alias database -- run it after editing aliases  *newmail       watch for mail arriving and say so  *printmail     format a message for a printer  *readmsg       print selected messages from a folder------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/UUCP -- mail and Usenet news over UUCP (18)------------------------------------------------------  UUCPbb 2.1, Bob Billson's UUCP for OS-9, with patch 1 applied to *expire and rnews.  This is a complete store-and-forward system: it  dials another machine over a serial line, exchanges mail and news  batches, and runs the jobs the other end queued for you.  Before the  internet reached most people, this WAS how mail arrived.  Kept in its own directory because these eighteen only make sense  together, and because they need a configuration of their own --  those same files are INSTALLED at /dd/SYS/UUCP, which is where the  programs look, so `uuname` already answers with the sample sites  rather than an error.  /dd/DOC/uucpbb/SAMPLE keeps the pristine  copies to go back to.  The manuals are in  /dd/DOC/uucpbb, already formatted; `list` them.  Every one is starred: they were built `cc -qixm` like most of this  collection's era, so supply your own cio.  Nothing here will do  anything useful under os9exec, which has no serial